% ---- standard sections ---------------------------------------------------------------
% \generalinstructions{solution-folder-name}
\newcommand{\generalinstructions}[1]{%
\section*{General Instructions}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Thorough Review:} Read the whole problem statement, including the theory, before writing any code. Do the short analytic derivations \emph{by hand first}; the code is there to confirm and extend your understanding, not to replace it.
  \item \textbf{Pacing:} This is a self-paced problem. The split below is a guide for the effort estimate in the header.
\end{itemize}
\begin{center}\small\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}clp{8.6cm}@{}}\toprule
\textbf{Stage} & \textbf{Phase} & \textbf{What you should have at the end of it}\\\midrule
1 & Think (${\sim}20\%$) & Theory understood; every analytic result derived; signals, units and sampling rates written down.\\
2 & Build (${\sim}30\%$) & Core code working end to end on the baseline data and validated against the analytic results.\\
3 & Experiment (${\sim}30\%$) & Full sweeps, stress tests and the real or synthetic datasets processed; first plots and numbers.\\
4 & Refine (${\sim}20\%$) & Weaknesses fixed, honest analysis and memo written, repository folder clean.\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{center}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Compute and Tools:} Everything is designed to run on a laptop CPU in Python 3.10+ (NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib, and where stated pandas, Astropy, scikit-learn, PyWavelets and PyTorch). No GPU and no hardware are required; where a problem offers an optional hardware experiment it is marked as a bonus. A full run should never need more than a few minutes; if yours does, vectorise before you scale up.
  \item \textbf{Data:} Each problem lists (i) a \emph{synthetic generator} from \texttt{sp\_common.synth} (offline, seeded, with known ground truth) and (ii) \emph{real datasets} to download yourself. Develop and validate on the synthetic data first, then repeat the key result on real data. Never commit large raw data files to the repository.
  \item \textbf{Submission Format:} Push your work to \texttt{solutions/#1/} in the repository: (i) clean, commented, reproducible Jupyter notebook(s) with fixed random seeds, (ii) a short written report (\texttt{report.pdf} or \texttt{report.md}) containing the requested analysis sections, and (iii) all figures in a \texttt{figures/} sub-folder. The report must be understandable without running the code.
  \item \textbf{Originality:} All code, analysis and written explanations must be your own work. Any external library, snippet, paper or AI tool you use must be cited in the report. You should be able to explain every line you submit.
  \item \textbf{Honesty over Hype:} Validate against analytic results \emph{before} trusting a number. A modest or negative result that is correctly analysed is worth more than an inflated claim. Report error bars, false-alarm rates and failure cases, not only your best run.
  \item \textbf{Units:} Write the units of every quantity and state in the first notebook cell whether a frequency is in $\si{\hertz}$ or $\si{\radian\per\second}$ and whether it is normalised to the sampling rate. Most mistakes in signal processing are factors of $2\pi$, factors of $2$ in one-sided spectra, or an off-by-one in the frequency axis.
  \item \textbf{Getting Stuck:} Re-read the theory section, check the self-check targets, then ask your mentor or open an issue in the repository. Describe what you expected, what you got, and what you already tried.
\end{itemize}}

% \evaluation : self-assessment rubric
\newcommand{\evaluation}{%
\section*{Evaluation}
Score your own submission out of 100 on five equally weighted criteria, then ask your mentor to score it independently and compare.
\begin{center}\small\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\bfseries}p{3.0cm}X r@{}}\toprule
Criterion & What to look for & Marks\\\midrule
Conceptual depth & Correct mathematics and physics; every result explained through a mechanism (why the spectrum looks like this), not only through code behaviour. & /20\\
Implementation quality & Correct, modular, commented, reproducible code; no unexplained magic numbers; analytic checks and self-check targets pass. & /20\\
Aerospace-application integration & The signal-processing result is carried through to a measurable consequence for a sensor, a link, a flight system or a detection decision, as the task requires. & /20\\
Analysis and critical thinking & Error bars, convergence, comparison to theory, honest treatment of limitations, false alarms and failure cases. & /20\\
Communication & Clear report, labelled plots (units, legends, captions) and a memo that a non-expert student could follow. & /20\\\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}\end{center}
Bonus tasks are not part of the 100 marks. A bonus attempted badly does not cost you marks, and a well-executed one is the best sign that you own the material.}
